        "title": "Refrigerants",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Refrigerant selection is driven by three competing concerns: thermodynamic performance,\nsafety (flammability/toxicity), and environmental impact (ozone depletion and climate).\nThe industry has been forced through three major transitions in 40 years, with a fourth\nunderway now."
    },
    {
      "id": "03-REFRIGERANTS.md#003",
      "path": [
        "03-REFRIGERANTS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:hvac:03-refrigerants, git-history:hvac:03-refrigerants]",
        "canonical_path": "hvac/03-REFRIGERANTS.md",
        "concepts": "[refrigerants]",
        "current_path": "hvac/03-REFRIGERANTS.md",
        "id": "maxim:hvac:refrigerants",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "hvac",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[refrigerants]",
        "section": "hvac",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Refrigerants",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    REFRIGERANT TRANSITION TIMELINE                   |\n|                                                                      |\n|  1930s–1980s:  CFCs dominant (R-12, R-11, R-502)                     |\n|                Great thermodynamics, zero flammability               |\n|                → Discovered: destroys stratospheric ozone            |\n|                                                                      |\n|  1987:   Montreal Protocol → CFC phaseout mandate                    |\n|  1996:   R-12 production ends in developed nations                   |\n|                                                                      |\n|  1990s–2000s:  HCFCs as transition (R-22 dominant)                   |\n|                Lower ODP than CFCs, still good thermodynamics        |\n|                → Also ozone-depleting (less so), and high GWP        |\n|                                                                      |\n|  2010:   R-22 new equipment banned in US                             |\n|  2020:   R-22 new production/import banned in US                     |\n|                                                                      |\n|  2006–2024:  HFCs dominant (R-410A, R-134a, R-404A)                  |\n|                ODP=0, excellent thermodynamics                       |\n|                → High GWP (climate forcing)                          |\n|                                                                      |\n|  2016:   Kigali Amendment to Montreal Protocol → HFC phasedown       |\n|  2025:   US GWP cap: new residential AC/HP must use ≤700 GWP         |\n|                                                                      |\n|  2025+:  A2L refrigerants (R-32, R-454B, R-466A) + naturals          |\n|                Low GWP, mildly flammable (A2L class)                 |\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
